#!/usr/bin/env bash
# pre-push - refuse to publish an installed Founder OS to a remote nobody has
# confirmed is private.
#
# Why this exists: an installed OS tracks the founder's identity, clients,
# leads and notes. One `git push` to a public repository publishes all of it,
# and that cannot be taken back - forks, caches and scrapers keep what a later
# delete removes. The pre-commit remote check only knows the public FounderOS
# repo by its address, so a push to any OTHER public repo went straight through.
#
# The rule: when core/identity.md is part of what this push can publish (an
# installed OS, not the product repo a developer works on), the push goes
# through only to the remote named in `git config founderos.privateRemote`,
# at the address in `git config founderos.privateRemoteUrl`. Both must match:
# a name alone can be re-pointed at a public repository with `git remote
# set-url`, and the confirmation would follow it there. The backup skill sets
# both after `gh repo view --json isPrivate` returns true, and never
# otherwise. A person can set them by hand once they have checked the
# repository is private themselves.
#
# Git passes the remote name as $1 (the URL itself when pushing to a bare
# URL) and the URL as $2. stdin carries one line per ref being pushed:
#   <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>
#
# Never prints anything on an allowed push. No Python needed.

remote_name="${1:-}"
remote_url="${2:-}"

# Personal data is in play when identity is tracked now, or when any commit
# being pushed carries it in its history (untracking a file does not take it
# out of the commits that already hold it).
has_identity=""
if git ls-files --error-unmatch -- core/identity.md >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  has_identity="1"
fi
publishes=""
while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
  [ -n "$local_sha" ] || continue
  case "$local_sha" in
    *[!0]*) ;;
    *) continue ;;   # all zeros: a branch delete, which publishes nothing
  esac
  publishes="1"
  if [ -z "$has_identity" ] \
     && [ -n "$(git rev-list -n 1 "$local_sha" -- core/identity.md 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
    has_identity="1"
  fi
done

# Every ref in this push is a delete: nothing of the founder's leaves the
# machine, so let it through. That is how a branch pushed somewhere public
# before this guard existed gets taken down again.
[ -n "$publishes" ] || exit 0

[ -n "$has_identity" ] || exit 0

confirmed="$(git config --get founderos.privateRemote 2>/dev/null || true)"
confirmed_url="$(git config --get founderos.privateRemoteUrl 2>/dev/null || true)"
if [ -n "$confirmed" ] && [ "$remote_name" = "$confirmed" ] \
   && [ -n "$confirmed_url" ] && [ "$remote_url" = "$confirmed_url" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

# A push straight to an address, with no remote name. Git then passes the
# address as both $1 and $2. Telling the founder to confirm the address as
# the remote NAME would replace the confirmation of their real remote (say
# origin), and their next ordinary push would be refused. So: name it first.
if [ "$remote_name" = "$remote_url" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<EOF

Push refused: this folder is your Founder OS, and this push goes straight to
an address with no remote name, which has not been confirmed private.

What is at risk: your identity, clients, leads and notes are in this
history. A push to a repository anyone can see publishes all of it, and a
public push cannot be taken back.

EOF
  if [ -n "$confirmed" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF
You already confirmed the remote "$confirmed" as private. If this address
is that repository, run the same push again with the name $confirmed in
place of the address.
If it is a different repository, do not push here. Say "back this up" and
the OS sets up a private one for you.
EOF
  else
    cat >&2 <<EOF
If you have checked the repository is private (on GitHub the page shows the
word Private next to its name), add it as a named remote first, confirm
that name and address once, then run the same push again with the name
backup in place of the address:
  git remote add backup "$remote_url"
  git config founderos.privateRemote backup
  git config founderos.privateRemoteUrl "$remote_url"
If it is public, do not push. Say "back this up" and the OS sets up a
private one for you.
EOF
  fi
  printf '\nRemote address: %s\n' "$remote_url" >&2
  exit 1
fi

cat >&2 <<EOF

Push refused: this folder is your Founder OS, and the remote "$remote_name"
at the address below has not been confirmed private.

What is at risk: your identity, clients, leads and notes are in this
history. A push to a repository anyone can see publishes all of it, and a
public push cannot be taken back.

How to check the repository is private:
  - On GitHub, open the repository page. A private one shows the word
    Private next to its name. Or run:
      gh repo view OWNER/REPO --json isPrivate
    and look for "isPrivate": true.
  - On any other site, check the repository's visibility setting.

If it is private, confirm it once, then push again:
  git config founderos.privateRemote $remote_name
  git config founderos.privateRemoteUrl "$remote_url"
The confirmation holds for that name at that address only. If the
address changes, the guard asks again.

If it is public, do not push. Make it private first, or say
"back this up" and the OS sets up a private one for you.

Remote address: $remote_url
EOF
exit 1
